WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:04.960
The most crucial and decisive battle of World War II is about to be fought.

00:00:05.620 --> 00:00:07.960
1943, Battle of Midway.

00:00:23.600 --> 00:00:31.500
And welcome to NEStalgia, a chronological exploration of every NES game released in North America.

00:00:31.720 --> 00:00:32.580
I'm Mike.

00:00:32.860 --> 00:00:33.720
I'm Sean.

00:00:33.980 --> 00:00:45.020
And I'm Joe. And guys, today we are playing what's not really like our first sequel, but it's like a sequel to a game that we really didn't like.

00:00:50.960 --> 00:00:52.240
You guys hated this one.

00:00:52.440 --> 00:00:53.800
I did. I hated it very much.

00:00:53.800 --> 00:00:59.680
Yeah, I was not a fan, particularly because I remember playing it as a kid and thought it was going to bring back all the nostalgia for me.

00:00:59.780 --> 00:01:01.680
And really, I just was frustrated.

00:01:01.680 --> 00:01:07.300
Yeah, it really, it really brought, put a bad taste in my mouth for all of the shmups to come.

00:01:08.160 --> 00:01:14.480
Yeah. And you know, it's funny because at this point now, like 1942 might have been our very first shmup.

00:01:14.480 --> 00:01:20.480
But now we've played so many of them and really just games from Capcom and Konami.

00:01:20.560 --> 00:01:22.960
They just continue to put like shmup after shmup.

00:01:23.100 --> 00:01:27.520
And Capcom, the last game we played from them was Legendary Wings.

00:01:27.580 --> 00:01:29.700
And I find that to be a very forgettable shmup.

00:01:29.780 --> 00:01:36.040
What about you guys? I didn't hate, I didn't hate Legendary Wings, but I also don't remember much about it.

00:01:36.040 --> 00:01:37.200
Yeah, the same as Joe.

00:01:38.140 --> 00:01:41.880
Um, but no, I was just gonna say, I don't remember too much about it.

00:01:41.920 --> 00:01:42.940
I remember being very similar.

00:01:43.140 --> 00:01:46.320
Like, it's just as if I remember being like a middle of the road.

00:01:47.080 --> 00:01:50.180
Yeah, it had like vertical and horizontal shmup levels.

00:01:50.440 --> 00:01:53.080
Yes, I do remember. You were like an angel.

00:01:53.980 --> 00:01:55.020
It just, I don't know.

00:01:55.220 --> 00:01:56.400
It wasn't a lot of connective.

00:01:56.500 --> 00:01:57.520
It flew into that thing's mouth, right?

00:01:57.640 --> 00:02:00.180
And there was like a bonus level in there and like things like that.

00:02:00.260 --> 00:02:01.100
Is that the one I'm thinking of?

00:02:01.100 --> 00:02:01.900
Yes.

00:02:01.900 --> 00:02:09.500
That's exactly the one. So we got rid of the beeps in 1943 here, but we also, we got rid of a lot of things.

00:02:09.640 --> 00:02:13.600
So I think just to give an overview of what 1943, the Battle of Midway is all about.

00:02:13.840 --> 00:02:20.900
The Battle of Midway was fought between the Imperial Japanese Navy, commanded by Yamamoto,

00:02:21.180 --> 00:02:24.040
and the United States Navy, commanded by Nimitz.

00:02:24.040 --> 00:02:30.720
And this battle took place just seven months following the attack on Pearl Harbor on June 4th, 1942.

00:02:31.220 --> 00:02:33.900
Right. Which is what gives it the name, 1943.

00:02:33.900 --> 00:02:34.960
We had hardcore history right now.

00:02:36.080 --> 00:02:39.640
I thought it would be funny if I gave an overview of the actual Battle of Midway.

00:02:39.760 --> 00:02:41.340
But it is, I think it is important.

00:02:41.340 --> 00:02:44.860
And now an interview with a Navy pilot from back then.

00:02:44.860 --> 00:02:52.940
I think it is important though, because in the history of it, we learned that it took place in 1942, not 1943.

00:02:53.580 --> 00:03:06.400
Which makes it just funny that they, their best option to name the sequel to 1942, not wanting to do 1942 to, they just named it the next year, which is not when the battle took place.

00:03:06.880 --> 00:03:13.760
That's great. I love that name in convention, but I don't understand, is the subtitle literally Battle of Midway?

00:03:13.900 --> 00:03:15.260
Because I don't see that written anywhere.

00:03:15.600 --> 00:03:21.740
See, I'm under the impression just looking at like the instructions manual, that it has it in there.

00:03:22.060 --> 00:03:27.040
I actually don't have the box art for reference, but I thought it was there.

00:03:27.300 --> 00:03:28.760
Yeah, it's definitely on the manual.

00:03:28.760 --> 00:03:35.260
The important thing too though, Sean, to your point, Sean, there is that this was an arcade game for a long time before it became an NES game.

00:03:35.380 --> 00:03:39.740
So it is possible that they didn't feel the need to include the Battle of Midway.

00:03:39.960 --> 00:03:45.100
But I also think in Japan, it wasn't referred to by its actual historical battle.

00:03:45.380 --> 00:03:49.200
And instead it was called like the Battle of Valhalla or something like that.

00:03:49.240 --> 00:03:51.840
Yeah, that would probably ruffle some feathers.

00:03:52.340 --> 00:03:53.320
Right. Yeah.

00:03:54.140 --> 00:04:02.860
But if it is, was originally an arcade game, is it possible that it did that thing that we've seen a lot of other things do where when it gets ported to the NES, it just gets like an arbitrary subtitle.

00:04:02.940 --> 00:04:08.860
Well, this one's not arbitrary, but just gets a subtitle to differentiate it or make it feel like a different experience.

00:04:09.540 --> 00:04:16.660
No, because I know it had Battle of Midway in the arcade because they wound up eventually releasing like Battle of Midway Mark II.

00:04:17.080 --> 00:04:20.000
Gotcha. They got very creative.

00:04:20.000 --> 00:04:21.260
They didn't just call it 1944?

00:04:22.540 --> 00:04:24.540
Or 1943 too?

00:04:24.780 --> 00:04:26.740
Yeah. It was Battlefield 1949.

00:04:27.480 --> 00:04:30.580
It should have been 1941.8 over two days.

00:04:32.000 --> 00:04:34.500
Oh, God. They're really limiting the...

00:04:34.500 --> 00:04:35.800
They're really 3.58 over two days.

00:04:36.680 --> 00:04:38.680
They are limiting themselves though.

00:04:39.120 --> 00:04:42.700
Because 1944, 45 is the end, right?

00:04:42.860 --> 00:04:46.120
So like you can only have like three games left at best.

00:04:46.120 --> 00:04:54.400
Well, I think that after that they would go like 1944, like January, and then like February.

00:04:54.680 --> 00:04:57.000
Yeah, 1944 January 1st.

00:04:57.040 --> 00:04:59.040
Then 1944 January 2nd.

00:04:59.920 --> 00:05:01.520
Yeah, it'd be fun.

00:05:01.520 --> 00:05:05.700
And in many ways, that is a Tetsuya Nomura naming convention.

00:05:07.500 --> 00:05:29.650
So we're not going to talk about the arcade game, but I think it's important for everyone to know that the NES version is the version of 1943 that introduces the mechanic that lets you improve your plane by divvying out like various progression points to your fuel gauge or your power or your defense in the arcade.

00:05:29.650 --> 00:05:31.570
In the arcade, none of that was there.

00:05:31.570 --> 00:05:43.350
You just had the standard plane that you found in 1942, and you just continuously kept going, you know, throughout each level, never upgrading or changing your ship.

00:05:44.570 --> 00:05:47.530
Yeah, I mean, I like the concept.

00:05:47.530 --> 00:05:56.690
I played through it a couple times with dispersing my skill points or whatever you want to say in different areas.

00:05:56.690 --> 00:05:58.750
I couldn't really feel any different.

00:05:58.990 --> 00:06:00.450
It didn't feel much different to me at all.

00:06:00.830 --> 00:06:03.650
But it's cool that it's there.

00:06:04.230 --> 00:06:10.770
Yeah, I felt like, I mean, I don't know if I experimented enough to really say which ones I thought were the best.

00:06:10.770 --> 00:06:17.110
But I ended up putting a lot of my my points into either offensive power or energy level.

00:06:17.830 --> 00:06:23.450
And I and I'm not sure how much use I would have gotten out of some like defensive power, which sounds like it would be helpful.

00:06:23.450 --> 00:06:27.110
But at least for the way I was playing the game, I don't think it would have helped me that much.

00:06:28.630 --> 00:06:36.610
Just because your your your defensive power like defends you until you get to like one hit left and then you die.

00:06:36.850 --> 00:06:39.610
And I spend most of the game on one hit left and then I die.

00:06:39.810 --> 00:06:42.890
So like it just postpones that a little bit for me.

00:06:43.570 --> 00:06:47.010
And I feel like offense was was the most useful for me.

00:06:47.310 --> 00:06:52.270
Yeah. So you guys didn't have like a specific loadout that you preferred when you started the game.

00:06:52.270 --> 00:07:07.270
They let you distribute three points across the five different categories, which are offensive and defensive power, your maximum fuel level and special weapons and how long those special weapons like the duration on them.

00:07:07.270 --> 00:07:09.690
I never touched the special weapons thing.

00:07:09.870 --> 00:07:23.250
But for me, I found that the maximum fuel level, the energy thing that Joe was referring to, I found that to be the most important thing because it's the only thing standing between you and this being a regular shmup where one hit kills you.

00:07:23.250 --> 00:07:43.430
Yeah, I also didn't touch the special gauge that much because everything about this game is so accurate as far as any S game can be up until the idea that this plane can like summon the power of weather and Zeus like lightning bolting you.

00:07:44.030 --> 00:07:47.290
So I just didn't I just chose not to do that for aesthetic reasons.

00:07:47.290 --> 00:07:53.050
Yeah, I preferred in 1942 when you got to just do like the barrel roll to get away from enemies.

00:07:53.490 --> 00:07:55.110
Yeah, you can still do that.

00:07:56.150 --> 00:08:02.070
I think if you use special too many times along those lines, it's the same button, I think.

00:08:02.230 --> 00:08:03.110
Yeah, but it but it does.

00:08:03.290 --> 00:08:04.610
It does make it.

00:08:04.990 --> 00:08:11.410
It almost incentivizes you not to ever use it to get away from enemies because then you waste one of your special attacks.

00:08:11.950 --> 00:08:16.930
Yeah, that's you know, I thought that was a little bit of a flaw not to not to.

00:08:16.930 --> 00:08:19.850
Praise 1942 over 1943 at all.

00:08:21.110 --> 00:08:31.250
So before we talk about like the actual game, I want to talk about this fuel gauge because it's the most interesting part of this shmup, the one that differentiates it from all the other ones that we played on the NES so far.

00:08:31.250 --> 00:08:38.770
And the idea basically is as we talked about here is that it was in it was think of the fuel gauge as your HP level.

00:08:39.270 --> 00:08:42.850
So if you have 99, you it counts down from there.

00:08:43.050 --> 00:08:50.010
So you whether you're playing or not similar to that Dragon Ball game on the NES where like no matter what you do, the HP just keeps going down.

00:08:50.190 --> 00:08:52.950
But it also goes down if you get hit by something.

00:08:52.950 --> 00:08:54.950
So you don't just take one hit in this shmup.

00:08:55.090 --> 00:09:00.070
You can theoretically take like seven or eight on a good day before you deplete your fuel.

00:09:00.310 --> 00:09:07.230
But even if your fuel is at zero, you still have an option to take on one more hit and then you'll be gone for good.

00:09:07.230 --> 00:09:13.030
My question for either of you is, was this was this really enough, though?

00:09:13.210 --> 00:09:17.870
Like I should like the fact that I don't it's not a one hit KO game.

00:09:18.050 --> 00:09:19.830
It allows you to endure multiple hits.

00:09:19.830 --> 00:09:27.090
But I really hated the fuel idea because the levels are the levels are very short bursts of interactivity.

00:09:27.270 --> 00:09:30.850
So the idea that your plane would be out of fuel seems kind of silly.

00:09:30.850 --> 00:09:37.650
And couple that with tying it to something like HP and something that just continuously falls down.

00:09:37.850 --> 00:09:38.930
It really doesn't work out.

00:09:39.010 --> 00:09:44.290
I never really found myself being able to manage my HP to be high enough by the time I got to a boss battle.

00:09:44.790 --> 00:09:45.750
I think.

00:09:46.110 --> 00:09:47.190
Go ahead. Go ahead, Jeff.

00:09:47.430 --> 00:09:54.330
I was just going to say I completely agree that it was I think it's a really cool idea and it should be something that works better.

00:09:54.330 --> 00:10:02.130
But I think that that coupled with the fact that your fuel gauge or HP or whatever you want to call it is constantly draining no matter what.

00:10:02.370 --> 00:10:07.670
Like it's almost like a time limit that makes it so like almost always if you get fuel.

00:10:08.850 --> 00:10:11.130
You'll run out of it by the time you find more fuel.

00:10:11.270 --> 00:10:18.110
So like I feel like once you get to zero, you're never in that level going to get back up to max or to more than a few hits.

00:10:18.270 --> 00:10:19.690
I thought that it was fine.

00:10:20.330 --> 00:10:34.250
The thing I was very critical of the draining health gauge in in the Dragon Ball game because it just reaching zero is game over for you.

00:10:34.510 --> 00:10:36.230
Like that didn't matter what you were doing.

00:10:36.390 --> 00:10:42.130
Didn't matter if you were like a pixel away from picking up some replenishment or the end of a level.

00:10:42.290 --> 00:10:47.250
It just ended right there because you were out of life time, whatever unit.

00:10:48.990 --> 00:10:58.930
But in this game, if it gets to zero or zero ish, like you can just sort of cruise there and sort of act like a normal shmup where if you get hit, you die.

00:10:59.270 --> 00:11:05.590
So I think that it was an all right amount of leniency on the game's part.

00:11:06.810 --> 00:11:11.330
I mean, really, the fact that it drains, I guess, isn't that important to me.

00:11:11.930 --> 00:11:23.350
It's just if you can't get those red planes that if you get all of them, it drops some energy, you're kind of shit out of luck.

00:11:23.490 --> 00:11:28.890
So it makes you more wary of picking up energy pickups when you can.

00:11:29.330 --> 00:11:32.270
Yeah. But I didn't have the same problems you guys had with it.

00:11:32.270 --> 00:11:47.610
See, because I do still feel like that you could accomplish that, though, without because it's a shmup and because, I mean, probably the regular player gets hit enough times where I think you're still going to want to like manage your energy level as much as possible.

00:11:47.890 --> 00:11:51.550
Whereas when it's just constantly draining, it almost feels like you have very little control.

00:11:51.790 --> 00:11:53.370
It's just when you find an energy thing, pick it up.

00:11:53.590 --> 00:11:57.130
But it's going to drain whether you're playing good or you're playing bad.

00:11:57.130 --> 00:12:06.710
So it felt like after the first 30 seconds of the level, you're back to playing just pretty much a regular shmup with like a little boost once in a while.

00:12:07.730 --> 00:12:14.110
Not saying that it's anywhere near as bad as Dragon Ball was, where like at least this doesn't completely hinder you.

00:12:14.190 --> 00:12:15.710
You can still play this as a regular shmup.

00:12:17.430 --> 00:12:25.050
But that's what made me feel like the intent that they had behind putting it in there was kind of lost because...

00:12:25.050 --> 00:12:29.270
I guess some of it could be like the...

00:12:29.270 --> 00:12:40.290
We're seeing more of the negative here because we see the fuel as like your baseline should be 64 or 99 or whatever your max is.

00:12:40.470 --> 00:12:45.830
But really, any amount that you have over 20 is sort of a bonus.

00:12:45.830 --> 00:12:54.650
So if it's draining, it's just like you may as well be a bit more risky while you have this extra life than you would otherwise.

00:12:55.110 --> 00:12:57.950
I don't know. I guess it just depends on how you want to look at it.

00:12:58.130 --> 00:13:00.430
Yeah, that's fair. I'll agree with you there, Sean.

00:13:00.490 --> 00:13:03.870
I think that's an interesting thing that I didn't consider not being a pro player.

00:13:03.870 --> 00:13:11.710
But if you're a pro player of shmups, having the fuel gauge not countdown might almost make the game too easy.

00:13:12.630 --> 00:13:19.010
And so this kind of keeps it at a level playing field and almost is like a fun advantage for newbies,

00:13:19.010 --> 00:13:22.790
but also something that they have to remember to deal with.

00:13:52.510 --> 00:14:03.790
I think that's a good thing.

00:14:09.630 --> 00:14:14.790
You were talking about the trade-off in the power-ups that you get from the Red Plains,

00:14:14.910 --> 00:14:20.330
which is like a traditional shmup trope where like a series of enemies come down that are slightly different colored.

00:14:20.590 --> 00:14:24.630
And, you know, if you kill all of them, you'll get this power-up to appear on screen.

00:14:24.670 --> 00:14:28.010
And I like how that's communicated now in shmups, just like a untold law.

00:14:28.450 --> 00:14:29.850
You know, how do these things happen?

00:14:29.850 --> 00:14:34.150
How do all developers like communicate with each other and say, this is what we're going to do?

00:14:34.290 --> 00:14:36.850
It's called sublimation.

00:14:38.190 --> 00:14:39.210
Devastation. Got it.

00:14:39.550 --> 00:14:42.070
And so once you kill all those Red Plains,

00:14:42.770 --> 00:14:46.510
once you kill all those Red Plains and you get like a POW symbol or something,

00:14:46.730 --> 00:14:51.010
you could just take that or you can keep shooting at it and you can get,

00:14:51.830 --> 00:15:02.890
you can turn it into a spread weapon and you can also turn it into like a larger amount of fuel until a certain point where it just like is a permanent,

00:15:03.070 --> 00:15:05.590
like it can no longer be changed into anything anymore.

00:15:05.810 --> 00:15:08.330
And it's like the maximum amount of fuel you can collect.

00:15:08.550 --> 00:15:10.530
That is something I didn't even notice.

00:15:10.550 --> 00:15:13.150
I didn't know that you could keep shooting it and it would morph.

00:15:13.150 --> 00:15:14.750
I just picked it up immediately.

00:15:15.310 --> 00:15:16.630
Oh, it's funny. Yeah. It's really weird.

00:15:16.630 --> 00:15:20.470
As you, as you keep shooting it, it changes like its icon a little bit.

00:15:20.630 --> 00:15:25.570
And then it ends with like what looks like two fuel canisters as the final thing.

00:15:25.610 --> 00:15:27.130
I thought I might be wrong,

00:15:27.150 --> 00:15:29.810
but I thought that once you get those two fuel canisters,

00:15:29.970 --> 00:15:33.470
if you keep shooting it, you'll turn it back into the POW symbol.

00:15:34.150 --> 00:15:39.110
Oh, you know, I never shot beyond that because that it didn't keep moving forward when I shot it,

00:15:39.210 --> 00:15:41.990
but that's something that might be true.

00:15:42.150 --> 00:15:43.510
I didn't, can you confirm that?

00:15:43.550 --> 00:15:45.250
I'm pretty sure I did it earlier.

00:15:45.550 --> 00:15:46.950
I was playing a game to refresh myself.

00:15:47.110 --> 00:15:51.090
Can you prove it right now, Joe? Yeah, I have, I have proof, but, uh, but you can't see it.

00:15:51.350 --> 00:15:52.570
Uh, I'm pretty sure I did it earlier.

00:15:52.690 --> 00:15:56.970
I can't, I can't confirm, but that's, that's how my memory works.

00:15:57.490 --> 00:15:59.610
I liked the spread weapon too.

00:15:59.610 --> 00:16:04.250
Um, the one that shoots out like three directional, um, ammo.

00:16:04.430 --> 00:16:08.950
But again, that is tied to like a limited amount, uh, of like,

00:16:09.030 --> 00:16:11.690
you only get like 12 of these in your capacity.

00:16:12.150 --> 00:16:14.390
And like, yeah, that happens in like,

00:16:14.750 --> 00:16:17.330
in terms of ammo in games and stuff like that.

00:16:17.370 --> 00:16:20.330
And we've even seen that in like guns, uh, gun dot smoke.

00:16:20.690 --> 00:16:21.630
Uh, but you know,

00:16:21.670 --> 00:16:23.690
I just thought it was weird here because in a shmup,

00:16:23.770 --> 00:16:25.470
the whole idea is like, once you have that power up,

00:16:25.570 --> 00:16:28.030
that's like, now your ship's a little more bad-ass.

00:16:28.030 --> 00:16:30.850
Now it's like, okay, your ship's a little more bad-ass for eight shots.

00:16:31.230 --> 00:16:34.970
Yeah, I guess this game isn't really going for the same power fantasy

00:16:34.970 --> 00:16:36.490
that something like Gradius is.

00:16:36.970 --> 00:16:40.150
Um, what they're going for instead, I'm not really sure.

00:16:40.430 --> 00:16:41.250
Um, but.

00:16:41.330 --> 00:16:44.210
This is the gritty World War II game that we've all been craving for.

00:16:44.310 --> 00:16:48.410
Yeah, exactly. Uh, yeah, I didn't mind the, you know, the, the different,

00:16:48.830 --> 00:16:50.630
the power up only lasting for a little while.

00:16:50.850 --> 00:16:53.130
You know, I think we're just used to like, yeah,

00:16:53.210 --> 00:16:57.090
the ability to customize or add new things and keep them until you get hit.

00:16:57.090 --> 00:16:58.830
Um, but that didn't bother me.

00:16:58.910 --> 00:17:01.250
It's, it just, it's almost like getting, it's not the same,

00:17:01.330 --> 00:17:04.870
but it's like, it's like getting a, the star in Mario

00:17:04.870 --> 00:17:06.870
or just getting something that gives you like a temporary boost

00:17:06.870 --> 00:17:08.170
in any number of games.

00:17:08.330 --> 00:17:11.630
Joe, what did you think about the, the enemies throughout the game?

00:17:11.790 --> 00:17:14.510
I thought it was pretty nice to have different sizes

00:17:14.510 --> 00:17:17.830
of enemy planes, uh, constantly on the screen with you.

00:17:17.910 --> 00:17:21.510
So you'll have like some really big ships that can offload smaller,

00:17:21.510 --> 00:17:24.250
sorry, really big planes that can offload smaller planes.

00:17:24.490 --> 00:17:27.890
And then you have bigger planes that shoot ammo back at you.

00:17:28.150 --> 00:17:32.670
You have, uh, boats that, uh, can attack from, uh,

00:17:32.970 --> 00:17:35.050
that thankfully you don't have to like, you don't have to bomb them.

00:17:35.190 --> 00:17:37.030
You can just shoot them. What did you think of all that?

00:17:37.190 --> 00:17:39.010
Uh, I thought it was, I thought it was a nice variety.

00:17:39.270 --> 00:17:41.430
I mean, to be honest, the thing that stuck out to me the most

00:17:41.430 --> 00:17:43.710
about the enemy variety or about the enemies in general

00:17:43.710 --> 00:17:46.430
is that they did something that I feel like you don't see

00:17:46.430 --> 00:17:47.750
in a lot of shmups where they don't just,

00:17:48.530 --> 00:17:50.910
you don't just fly by you and then are gone forever.

00:17:50.910 --> 00:17:53.190
They like fly up to you in like a formation

00:17:53.190 --> 00:17:54.990
